links highlight the clinically important tissue interfaces where abnormal cells develop and where diagnostic sampling commonly focuses. [Data: Entities (4617, 4602, 2062); Relationships (9871, 9866)]\\n\\nA specific sub-site is additionally emphasized: the ENDOCERVICAL CANAL is linked to cervical cancer with an estimate that about 2 out of 10 cervical cancers form in this canal, reinforcing that clinically relevant disease can originate beyond the ectocervix. [Data: Entities (4620); Relationships (9944)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"FIGO stage categories structure the community’s view of progression from early to locally advanced cervical cancer\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes a mapped set of FIGO stage entities for cervical cancer (Stage 1A through 3B), all explicitly connected to CERVICAL CANCER. This structure indicates that stage stratification is a primary organizing principle for decision-making and treatment selection in this knowledge network. [Data: Entities (4707, 4708, 4709, 4710, 4711, +more); Relationships (10186, 10187, 10188, 10189, 10190, +more)]\\n\\nLocoregional extension is specifically represented via named anatomy and stage definitions. PARAMETRIUM is included as a clinically important tissue surrounding the uterus/cervix, and Stage 2B cervical cancer is defined by growth into the parametrium, making parametrial assessment a clear pivot point between earlier and more locally advanced disease descriptions. [Data: Entities (4604, 4714); Relationships (9914, 10191)]\\n\\nMore advanced local spread is represented by PELVIC WALL involvement, which is directly linked to Stage 3B cervical cancer. This provides a defined escalation endpoint within the included staging framework that can influence the intensity and modality of therapy. [Data: Entities (4697, 4716); Relationships (10176, 10193)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Lymph node pathways and node assessment methods are central to staging and treatment planning\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community identifies nodal spread patterns and corresponding assessment approaches. PELVIC NODES are explicitly linked as the first nodal basin to which cervical cancer generally spreads, signaling why pelvic nodal evaluation is a frequent clinical priority. [Data: Entities (4758, 2062); Relationships (10318)]\\n\\nMultiple node-evaluation procedures are included. LYMPH NODE DISSECTION (LYMPHADENECTOMY) is connected to cervical cancer as a surgical removal of lymph nodes for testing, and LYMPH NODE TESTING (PATHOLOGIC EXAMINATION) is linked to removed LYMPH NODES being tested to determine spread. Together, these relationships describe a procedural chain from removal to pathology-based decision support. [Data: Entities (4762, 4786); Relationships (10338, 10317)]\\n\\nSURGICAL STAGING is presented as a broader pre-treatment strategy to determine true anatomic extent by sampling tissues and testing for cancer cells; within cervical cancer, it is described as often focusing on lymph node involvement and typically including para-aortic lymph node removal (with possible pelvic node removal). PARA-AORTIC NODES are separately defined as abdominal lymph nodes, reinforcing the “extended field” concept when evaluating more advanced spread. [Data: Entities (2198, 4759); Relationships (3790, 10320)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Fertility preservation is a distinct decision pathway contrasted with definitive reproductive-organ surgery\",\n      \"explanation\": \"FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT is defined as a management approach for selected low-risk, early-stage cancers—particularly certain stage 1 cervical cancers—intended to preserve reproductive potential and the option of future pregnancy, sometimes including careful observation in very low-risk situations. It is explicitly framed as requiring individualized discussion with the care team, highlighting shared decision-making as part of the pathway. [Data: Entities (4676); Relationships (10113)]\\n\\nThe community contains key reproductive-organ entities that contextualize fertility impact. The UTERUS is described as the organ where pregnancy develops, and its preservation is explicitly tied to maintaining the ability to carry a pregnancy—an essential rationale for fertility-sparing approaches. [Data: Entities (1988); Relationships (3360)]\\n\\nBy contrast, HYSTERECTOMY is defined as removal of the uterus and is described as permanently eliminating the ability to carry a pregnancy in the uterus. In parallel, BILATERAL SALPINGO-OOPHORECTOMY (BSO) is defined as removal of both ovaries (and tubes), which causes infertility due to loss of ovaries; this provides a clear depiction of how different surgical options can affect both gestation and hormonal/reproductive function. [Data: Entities (2187, 2189, 1510); Relationships (3756, 3761)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"External beam radiation therapy (EBRT) is detailed as a machine-delivered, fractionated, conformal treatment with defined side effects\",\n      \"explanation\": \"EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY (EBRT) is described as delivering radiation from outside the body via a machine, using a RADIATION BEAM aimed at a target such as the cancer site/tumor area. The presence of multiple connected entities (radiation machine, large radiation machine, radiation beam) emphasizes the operational, equipment-dependent nature of this modality. [Data: Entities (634, 617, 4772, 2738); Relationships (861, 5049, 10548)]\\n\\nDose delivery is formalized through FRACTIONS (small individual doses) and RADIATION DOSE (quantified prescribed amount), with the TREATMENT PLAN specifying the radiation dose(s). This establishes a planning-to-delivery chain that is central to safe and effective radiotherapy. [Data: Entities (4784, 4791); Relationships (10349, 10398)]\\n\\nEBRT is described as conformal (beams shaped to match the tumor), and common side effects within the community include SKIN REDNESS AND IRRITATION and FREQUENT OR PAINFUL URINATION. These links highlight both the technical intent (normal-tissue sparing through conformation) and the practical patient-impact considerations (toxicity monitoring). [Data: Entities (6132, 4782, 4783); Relationships (14024, 10353, 10356)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Radiation beam types (photons vs electrons) are differentiated by tumor depth and treatment context\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community distinguishes radiation beam modalities used in EBRT. PHOTONS are described as commonly used for deeper, more invasive tumors, while ELECTRONS are described as commonly used for superficial tumors. This provides a simplified but actionable mapping between beam choice and clinical geometry. [Data: Entities (621, 620, 649, 648); Relationships (868, 867)]\\n\\nThese beam types are integrated into the broader EBRT concept via direct relationships linking both PHOTONS and ELECTRONS to EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY (EBRT). This positioning indicates that modality selection is treated as a standard component of EBRT planning in the represented knowledge. [Data: Entities (621, 620, 634); Relationships (868, 867)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"IMRT is represented as an advanced EBRT technique intended to improve targeting and reduce collateral dose\",\n      \"explanation\": \"INTENSITY-MODULATED RADIATION THERAPY (IMRT) is described as an advanced form of external beam radiation therapy that uses many small beams with varying intensities to better spare normal tissues while delivering a high targeted dose to the tumor. It is explicitly framed as a recommended and preferred radiation technique in the context of anal cancer due to potential reductions in side effects by limiting exposure to nearby organs. [Data: Entities (4766); Relationships (10364)]\\n\\nIMRT is also directly linked as a technique of RADIATION THERAPY, with an operational caveat that it may not be offered at a given facility and may require referral depending on availability and care pathways. This positions IMRT not only as a technical capability but also as a service-access consideration within care networks. [Data: Entities (4766); Relationships (10364)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Risk factors, symptom elicitation, and treatment monitoring appear as supporting clinical processes around the core care pathway\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes selected risk factors associated with cervical cancer: ORAL CONTRACEPTIVE (BIRTH CONTROL) use and GIVING BIRTH (MULTIPARITY) are both explicitly linked as risk factors. These elements frame upstream clinical history-taking and population risk stratification as part of the broader information environment. [Data: Entities (4624, 4630, 2062); Relationships (9961, 9974)]\\n\\nSymptom screening is represented via WATERY VAGINAL DISCHARGE, linked as an example symptom doctors may ask about when evaluating for cervical cancer. This anchors the role of patient-reported symptoms within the diagnostic workflow described by the entity set. [Data: Entities (4663, 2062); Relationships (10021)]\\n\\nDownstream of diagnosis and treatment initiation, MONITORING DURING CHEMOTHERAPY is included, describing periodic physical exams and additional tests as needed, and explicitly linking physical exams every 1 to 3 cycles as part of monitoring. This indicates ongoing surveillance and toxicity/response assessment as a standard supporting process in systemic therapy contexts. [Data: Entities (2279); Relationships (3979)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 6.0,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity is moderate-high because the community represents clinically consequential cancer diagnosis, staging, and treatment decision pathways (surgery, radiation, and fertility preservation) that can materially affect patient outcomes.\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "us